From 9ed0c9a7b2720961ada636ceec1097dc63e22645 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 06:14:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Expire a gateway's boot-snapshot rows once it
 answers a live list

An archived hub chat (20261102_175427_594451, "Add DoorDash to Babybear")
kept disappearing and coming back in the MacBook sidebar. The hub's state.db
and replica both said archived=1.

Evidence (MacBook desktop.log, Desktop pid started Nov 11 00:55 PT, archive
override hlc 1794454508520 = 19:35 PT):
- every `stripe-transition 20261102_175427_594451 ... source=fallback` line
  (20:06:58-20:07:05, 21:45:37) sits inside a hub-stale window
  (`[session-freshness] hermes-hub/*:true->false reason=gateway-unavailable`
  at 20:06:32 / 21:44:54, back to true at 20:07:05 / 21:45:46);
- `source=fallback` = a row carrying offline_synced_at (replica/boot row),
  which is why host.sessions.rows ($sessions) never held it;
- the MacBook hub.db replica row and hub-sync /sessions exclude it
  (archived=1), so the only copy left was the launch-time boot snapshot.

Five whys:
1. Why did the archived row reappear? mergeOfflineFallback added a
   fallback row for it whenever the hub's list freshness was not true.
2. Why was there a fallback row for an archived chat? $sidebarFallbackSessions
   appends every boot-snapshot row the hub-sync plugin does not carry, and
   the plugin (correctly) drops archived rows.
3. Why did the boot snapshot still list it? It is read once from hub.db at
   launch (00:55) and never updated; the chat was archived at 19:35.
4. Why were stale boot rows still eligible 19 h after launch? Only the
   primary's own boot rows expire once their backend answers
   (ownBackendAnswered); a remote owner's boot rows never expire, so each
   time the hub went stale (busy backend during turns/notices: dispatch
   probe timeouts, failed lists) they filled the gap again.
5. Why no guard? The boot snapshot was designed as a cold-start stand-in,
   but "cold start is over for this owner" was only encoded for the primary.

Fix: record every owner whose live list was fresh at least once this launch
($answeredSessionOwners, fed by publishFreshness). Boot rows of an answered
owner are dropped from the fallback inputs for good; its live rows and the
hub-sync replica are newer truth. Owners that never answered keep their
boot rows, so the cold-start paint is unchanged.

Tests: boot-snapshot.test.ts covers the reappearance path (answered, then
stale via a failed list and via gateway-unavailable: row stays out; red on
the old source) and the never-answered control (boot row kept).
